          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Blaise Pascal</strong><br>\nBlaise Pascal was born on June 19, 1623, in Clermont-Ferrand, France. His mother died when he was only three years old, and his father, a tax collector with a passion for science and mathematics, educated him at home. Pascal showed extraordinary talent from an early age, crafting a treatise on sounds at age twelve and a mathematical theorem by sixteen.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Contributions to Mathematics</strong><br>\nPascal made significant contributions to the field of mathematics. He is best known for Pascal's Triangle, a simple but powerful tool that shows the coefficients of the expansion of a binomial expression. He also contributed to the study of probability and projective geometry, laying the groundwork for future developments in these areas.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Pascal's Work on Pressure</strong><br>\nIn addition to mathematics, Pascal studied physics, specifically the principles of fluid mechanics and hydrodynamics. He performed pioneering experiments on pressure, demonstrating how the height of a column of mercury was related to atmospheric pressure. This work led to the development of the barometer and advancements in understanding atmospheric pressure.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
