          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Invention of the Pascaline</strong><br>\nPascal was not only a theorist but also an inventor. He created the Pascaline, an early mechanical calculator capable of addition and subtraction, to help his father with tax calculations. It was a significant step forward in the development of computing and mechanical arithmetic.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Pascal's Wager</strong><br>\nPascal also explored religious philosophy and wrote about the rationality of faith in God. His famous concept, Pascal's Wager, argues that believing in God is the most pragmatic decision, as the potential gains from belief far outweigh any possible benefits from disbelief.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Correspondence with Pierre de Fermat</strong><br>\nPascal's correspondence with fellow mathematician Pierre de Fermat laid the foundation for the theory of probability. Their letters discussed the problems of gambling and mathematical expectation, leading to a systematic approach to probability that has influenced countless fields, from finance to science.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
